--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/deployment/GITHUB_ACTIONS_OIDC_SETUP.md
@@ -0,0 +1,115 @@
+# GitHub Actions → AWS OIDC setup (one-time, AWS console)
+
+`.github/workflows/deploy-ec2.yml` deploys to the free-tier EC2 box with
+**zero stored AWS credentials** — no access key/secret sits in a GitHub
+secret. It authenticates by exchanging a short-lived GitHub OIDC token for a
+temporary AWS role session. This costs nothing extra; it's an IAM feature.
+
+This only needs to be done once, before the workflow can run successfully.
+It assumes the EC2 instance from `docs/AWS_FREE_TIER_RUNBOOK.md` already
+exists, tagged `Name=luxgen-prod` (or set `EC2_INSTANCE_TAG` below to match
+whatever you used), with the SSM agent running (Amazon Linux 2023 ships with
+it) and an app checkout at `/opt/luxgen`.
+
+## 1. Add the GitHub OIDC identity provider
+
+**IAM → Identity providers → Add provider**
+
+- Provider type: `OpenID Connect`
+- Provider URL: `https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com`
+- Audience: `sts.amazonaws.com`
+
+(Skip this step if a `token.actions.githubusercontent.com` provider already
+exists in the account — one provider covers every repo.)
+
+## 2. Create the deploy role
+
+**IAM → Roles → Create role → Web identity**
+
+- Identity provider: the one from step 1
+- Audience: `sts.amazonaws.com`
+- Trust policy — edit after creation to scope it to this repo/branch only
+  (replace `<ACCOUNT_ID>` and `<GITHUB_ORG>`):
+
+```json
+{
+  "Version": "2012-10-17",
+  "Statement": [{
+    "Effect": "Allow",
+    "Principal": {
+      "Federated": "arn:aws:iam::<ACCOUNT_ID>:oidc-provider/token.actions.githubusercontent.com"
+    },
+    "Action": "sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity",
+    "Condition": {
+      "StringEquals": {
+        "token.actions.githubusercontent.com:aud": "sts.amazonaws.com"
+      },
+      "StringLike": {
+        "token.actions.githubusercontent.com:sub": "repo:<GITHUB_ORG>/luxgen-monorepo:ref:refs/heads/main"
+      }
+    }
+  }]
+}
+```
+
+The `sub` condition means only workflow runs triggered from `main` in this
+exact repo can assume the role — a PR from a fork, or a push to any other
+branch, cannot.
+
+- Permissions policy — attach an inline policy scoped to exactly what the
+  deploy needs, nothing broader:
+
+```json
+{
+  "Version": "2012-10-17",
+  "Statement": [
+    {
+      "Effect": "Allow",
+      "Action": "ec2:DescribeInstances",
+      "Resource": "*"
+    },
+    {
+      "Effect": "Allow",
+      "Action": ["ssm:SendCommand"],
+      "Resource": [
+        "arn:aws:ec2:*:<ACCOUNT_ID>:instance/*",
+        "arn:aws:ssm:*::document/AWS-RunShellScript"
+      ]
+    },
+    {
+      "Effect": "Allow",
+      "Action": ["ssm:GetCommandInvocation", "ssm:ListCommands"],
+      "Resource": "*"
+    }
+  ]
+}
+```
+
+- Name the role e.g. `luxgen-gha-deploy-role` and note its ARN.
+
+## 3. Set repository variables (not secrets — none of this is sensitive)
+
+**Repo → Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions → Variables tab**
+
+| Variable | Value |
+|---|---|
+| `AWS_DEPLOY_ROLE_ARN` | ARN of the role from step 2 |
+| `AWS_REGION` | `us-east-1` (or wherever the EC2 instance lives) |
+| `EC2_INSTANCE_TAG` | the `Name` tag on the instance, e.g. `luxgen-prod` |
+
+Nothing above is a secret — an assumable role ARN and a region string carry
+no credential material, which is the entire point of OIDC over long-lived
+access keys.
+
+## 4. (Optional) require manual approval before deploy
+
+`deploy-ec2.yml` targets `environment: production`. If you want a manual
+"approve to deploy" gate: **Repo → Settings → Environments → production →
+Required reviewers** → add yourself. Free on public repos.
+
+## 5. First run
+
+Push to `main` (CI workflow completes) or trigger
+**Actions → Deploy to EC2 (free tier) → Run workflow** manually. Check the
+run logs — the "Send deploy command over SSM" step's output mirrors exactly
+what ran on the instance, so a failure there is diagnosable without SSH.
